messages:
Marco V: Quick Q — working on Node SDK streaming from our hosted API (SSE). We're seeing JSON objects split across chunks so parse fails sometimes (e.g. chunk boundary in middle of a string or between braces). What are folks using for a robust incremental parser + simple backpressure in Node and the browser? Also curious if the SDK should expose a small helper for this.

Alyssa D: TL;DR: buffer + boundary-detection + pause/resume. For SSE we usually strip the `data:` prefix, accumulate text, and only JSON.parse when we detect an entire object. Two practical strategies: NDJSON (one JSON per line) or brace-depth counting. NDJSON is simplest if you control the producer. If not, depth counting works.

Alyssa D: Node example (very small):

```js
// incoming is an http.IncomingMessage from fetch/axios-like stream
const decoder = new TextDecoder('utf-8');
let buf = '';
let depth = 0;
let inString = false;
let escape = false;

incoming.on('data', chunk => {
  const text = decoder.decode(chunk, { stream: true });
  buf += text.replace(/^data: /gm, ''); // strip SSE prefix if present

  for (let i = 0; i < buf.length; i++) {
    const ch = buf[i];
    if (!inString) {
      if (ch === '{') depth++;
      if (ch === '}') depth--;
    }
    if (ch === '"' && !escape) inString = !inString;
    escape = (ch === '\\' && !escape);

    if (depth === 0 && !inString && buf.trim()) {
      try {
        // find first complete object and parse it
        const objText = buf.trim();
        const obj = JSON.parse(objText);
        handleObj(obj);
        buf = ''; // reset buffer after successful parse
      } catch (e) {
        // still partial or malformed — wait for more
      }
      break; // avoid re-parsing multiple times in this quick example
    }
  }
});
```

Alyssa D: For backpressure in Node streams, call `incoming.pause()` at the top of the data handler if downstream processing is slow, and `incoming.resume()` when you're ready. Also tune the readable stream `highWaterMark` if you need to control internal buffering. Example pattern: pause -> process async -> resume in finally.

Jia L: A few extra tips: 1) If you can change the server, switch to NDJSON or SSE with JSON per event (one `data:` = one JSON). That removes a lot of brittle parsing. 2) If you can't, use a streaming JSON parser (e.g., clarinet, JSONStream) which handles tokenization and strings properly instead of rolling your own naive brace counter. 3) For Node SDK ergonomics: expose a small exported util like `createSSEJsonStream(reader)` that yields parsed objects or async iterator — keeps app code simple.

Sam R: Browser side pattern (fetch + ReadableStream) I've been using:

```js
const res = await fetch(url);
const reader = res.body.getReader();
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
let buf = '';
let done = false;
while (!done) {
  const { value, done: rDone } = await reader.read();
  done = rDone;
  if (value) {
    buf += decoder.decode(value, { stream: true });
    // strip `data:` lines and split by `\n\n` (SSE event boundary)
    const parts = buf.split(/\n\n/);
    buf = parts.pop() || '';
    for (const part of parts) {
      const line = part.replace(/^data: /gm, '').trim();
      if (!line) continue;
      try {
        const obj = JSON.parse(line);
        await handleObj(obj); // await to apply natural backpressure
      } catch (e) {
        // could be partial; push back into buf
        buf = part + '\n\n' + buf;
        break;
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Sam R: Note the `await handleObj(obj)` in the browser loop — if you `await` processing you implicitly create backpressure because you don't read the next chunk until you're done. In Node, you need to `pause()` the stream similarly.

Marco V: Awesome, thanks. Two Qs: (1) any simple library you'd recommend instead of homegrown bracedepth? (2) If we add `createSSEJsonStream` to the SDK, should it return an async iterator or emit events?

Jia L: 1) clarinet or oboe.js can do streaming parses; clarinet is tiny and token-based. 2) Prefer async iterator for modern JS — it composes well with `for await (const obj of createSSEJsonStream(...)) {}` and works both browser + Node with small wrapper.

Alyssa D: +1 on async iterator. We should also provide two helpers: `sseToNdjson()` for servers that can opt-in, and `sseStream(parserOptions)` that uses either NDJSON or depth-based fallback. I'll draft a tiny util and a test.

